.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM-GET-KEY 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm-get-key \- show one encryption key's description
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekm get-key
.BR \-\-key | \-k
.I NAME\fR|\fIERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows one key's description as JSON: its ERN, the account and namespace it
belongs to, its algorithm and length, its status, its description and
tags, and when it was created and last modified.
.PP
The same description
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1)
gives of each of its own, for one key \(em so what a listing shows and
what this shows cannot drift apart.
.SH KEY MATERIAL
Never the key material. That has no way out of the key management module
at all, which is the point of having one:
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1)
are how a key is used, and the bytes stay inside EKM while they are.
.PP
So this is safe to print in a terminal, paste into a ticket, or run from
a script. What it reveals is that a key of that name exists and what it
is for.
.SH NAMES AND ERNS
A name is resolved in the session's own account and namespace. An ERN
names one key in the whole installation, and is what an encrypted bucket
or secret carries \(em the
.I encryptionKeyErn
printed by
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1)
can be pasted straight in here to find out which key a secret is
encrypted under, including one in another namespace that a bare name
would not reach.
.SH STATUS
The
.I status
is what distinguishes a key that can still be used from one that cannot.
A revoked or pending-deletion key still exists and is still returned
here; the status is the only thing that says so. Something that only needs
to know whether the name is taken wants
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-exists-key (1),
which answers as an exit code and does not distinguish them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fINAME\fR|\fIERN\fR
Name of the key in the session's account and namespace, or a full ERN.
Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The key was found and its description printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no key of that name, the caller may not read
it, or the server could not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli ekm get\-key \-\-key ess\-development

euclid\-cli ekm get\-key \e
    \-\-key ern:ekm:eu\-central\-1:000000000000:key:ess\-development
.fi
.RE
.PP
Which key a secret is encrypted under:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli ekm get\-key \-\-key "$(euclid\-cli ess get\-secret \e
    \-\-name db\-password | jq \-r .secret.encryptionKeyErn)"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-keys (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-create-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-exists-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-revoke-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-encrypt (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-decrypt (1)
